window.docContents[109].resolve({"/Hoare/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-I--Proof-Rules--Conditionals--Example":{"contents":"\n\ntheorem if_example :\n    {{True}}\n      if (X = 0) {\n        Y := 2\n      } else {\n        Y := X + 1\n      }\n    {{X ≤ Y}} := by\n  apply hoare_if\n  · -- Then\n    apply hoare_consequence_pre\n    · exact hoare_asgn\n    · assertion_auto\n  · -- Else\n    apply hoare_consequence_pre\n    · exact hoare_asgn\n    · assertion_auto\n\n\nif_example' (and the if_minus_plus solution below) write\napply hoare_consequence_pre hoare_asgn (by assertion_auto), supplying\nhoare_consequence_pre's two arguments directly instead of applying it\nand closing the two resulting goals separately. This idiom has\nnever been shown to students. Options: teach the general fact here with a\nsentence or two, or introduce the approach properly earlier in the text.\n===We can even shorten it a little bit more.theorem if_example' :\n    {{True}}\n      if (X = 0) {\n        Y := 2\n      } else {\n        Y := X + 1\n      }\n    {{X ≤ Y}} := by\n  apply hoare_if <;>\n    apply hoare_consequence_pre hoare_asgn (by assertion_auto)\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Hoare Logic\u0009Hoare: Hoare Logic, Part I\u0009Proof Rules\u0009Conditionals","header":"5.4.7.1. Example","id":"/Hoare/#Hoare-Logic--Hoare___-Hoare-Logic___-Part-I--Proof-Rules--Conditionals--Example"}});